    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "This report presents eqode, as well as its process of development and testing. The motivation for the project comes from the divide existing in technology education, which has been widened and exposed by the current context of distance learning. On the one hand, we see the rapid expansion of computer science classes in K-12 education, which affords some students the opportunity to learn how to code their first apps and software, and develop computational thinking skills. Most students, however, are left without the choice of becoming technologists: they don't identify with the standard image of the epistemological elite of computing; cutting edge coding curricula don't reach them; their teachers don't have the resources to guide them through their first steps in computational literacy. What efforts can be undertaken to help narrow this gap?\r\nDespite the fact that there is no panacea to this problem, this project explores one possible direction to make technology education accessible to students that schools continue to fail. This work is informed by critical pedagogy, explores constructionist practices, and leverages aspects of the design process to promote learning experiences that encourage youth to devise solutions to their surroundings while reflecting on the societal implications of technology. In other words, eqode is a curriculum framework that seeks to promote critical computational literacy.\r\nThe current version of this curriculum is the product of a collaborative process that involved two teenagers from underrepresented backgrounds and one educator who teaches the \"black, indigenous, marginalized youth\" in the outskirts of Belém, Northern Brazil. eqode also features videos that introduce topics in design and were produced by a youth-led film crew. The current iteration was informed by previous testing, including a pilot run conducted with two Brazilian teenagers that measured usability for teachers and learning outcomes for students.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
